{
  "abstract": "Can an actual greedy no-three-in-line process preserve its coarse available-site distribution without assuming independent availability? We construct a block-constant selection law that makes all sixteen available block proportions exact martingales. After a uniform start of $\\lfloor\\sqrt N\\rfloor$ steps, we prove that this law exists with site weights between $1/100$ and $9/5$ throughout every deterministic horizon $T=o(N/\\sqrt{\\log N})$, with probability tending to one. Its selected profile follows a continuum equation governed by the previously introduced cubic Radon functional. A full-dimensional projected-gradient identity gives exact exponential contraction and controls random asymmetric perturbations. We certify the entire positive continuum path with 1,632 rational interval boxes and supply separate independent checkers for its chord tensor and interval inclusions. The limiting sixteen-block profile has cubic energy strictly between $2.7881$ and $2.7883$, yielding an actual early-depletion coefficient approximately $0.847508$, versus $9/\\pi^2$ for uniform greedy. The stochastic proof uses adaptive site-cap bounds, within-block martingale centering, and an aggregate endpoint-deletion estimate; it assumes no product law for the deleted set. This establishes a rigorous spatial control mechanism, not a linear-time availability theorem or an improvement of the classical $1.5N$ construction.",
